# Syllogism: Possibility Cases and Venn Diagram Mastery

> Possibility questions flip syllogism from "what must be true" to
> "what could be true." This lesson covers the golden possibility rule,
> the Venn method, and the either-or and three-statement cases.

[important]
The golden rule: **a possibility conclusion is true when it is not
definitely contradicted** by the statements. You only have to fail to
disprove it. If every valid Venn arrangement contradicts it, the
possibility is false.
[/important]

## 1. Recap: The Four Statement Types

| Type | Form | Venn picture in words |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A — universal affirmative | All A are B | circle A sits fully inside circle B |
| E — universal negative | No A is B | circles A and B do not touch |
| I — particular affirmative | Some A are B | the circles overlap (A inside B also counts) |
| O — particular negative | Some A are not B | part of A sticks out of B |

[definition]
A **definite conclusion** must hold in *every* valid Venn arrangement.
A **possibility conclusion** only needs one arrangement in which it
*could* hold — it fails only if every arrangement contradicts it.
[/definition]

## 2. The Possibility Test in Three Steps

1. Draw every Venn arrangement the statements permit.
2. Ask: does any arrangement allow the conclusion's picture?
3. If none does, the statements definitely contradict it and the
   possibility fails; otherwise it is true.

## 3. Worked Possibility Cases

| Statement | Possibility conclusion | Verdict | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| All A are B | Some B being A is a possibility | True | not contradicted; some B are A is in fact definite here |
| Some A are not B | All A being B is a possibility | False | contradicted: the statement says some A lie outside B |
| Some A are B | All A being B is a possibility | True | "some" never excludes "all" |
| No A is B | Some A being B is a possibility | False | contradicted by the total separation |
| Some A are not B | Some A being B is a possibility | True | the A's outside B do not stop other A's being inside B |

[warning]
The phrase "is a possibility" changes the whole question. A conclusion
that fails as a definite claim may pass as a possibility, and a
reasonable-sounding one may fail because one arrangement contradicts it.
[/warning]

## 4. Conversion Rules Table

| Original statement | Valid conversion | Invalid conversion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| All A are B | Some B are A | All B are A |
| Some A are B | Some B are A | — |
| No A is B | No B is A | — |
| Some A are not B | no conversion exists | Some B are not A (does not follow) |

The O-type has no valid conversion — that single fact settles many trap
options instantly.

## 5. The Four Immediate-Inference Cases

| Statement | Definitely follows | Definitely fails |
| --- | --- | --- |
| All A are B | Some A are B; Some B are A | No A is B; Some A are not B |
| No A is B | Some A are not B; No B is A | Some A are B; All A are B |
| Some A are B | Some B are A | No A is B; All A are not B |
| Some A are not B | none beyond the original | All A are B |

**Worked case:** All parks are gardens. "Some gardens are parks" follows
(conversion of A-type), as does "Some parks are gardens" (A implies I);
"No park is a garden" and "Some parks are not gardens" both fail.

## 6. Three-Statement Syllogisms

With three statements, combine them **in pairs**:

```text
Statements: All A are B. All B are C. All C are D.
Pair 1+2 -> All A are C.   Pair 2+3 -> All B are D.
Combine:   All A are C + All C are D -> All A are D.
```

Derive every two-statement conclusion the rules allow, then treat those
derived conclusions as new statements and combine again — most three-
statement questions are two two-statement questions in one coat.

[tip]
Write the chain A → B → C → D over the terms. If the middle terms link
head-to-tail, the whole chain combines — the answer is about the ends.
[/tip]

## 7. Either-Or Cases (Complementary Pairs)

**Either-or** applies when two conclusions, taken alone, both fail — but
together they cover every arrangement. Conditions:

1. Both conclusions share the same subject and predicate.
2. One is universal, the other particular (an A-O pair or an E-I pair).
3. One is affirmative, the other negative.
4. Neither follows definitely from the statements.

**Worked case:** Some chairs are tables. Conclusions: I. All chairs are
tables. II. Some chairs are not tables. Neither follows definitely, but
they form an A-O complementary pair on the same terms — in every
arrangement exactly one is true. Answer: **either I or II follows**.

## 8. Two Full Worked Examples

**Example 1:** Some emails are letters. All letters are posts.
I. Some emails are posts — follows (Some + All combines to Some A are C).
II. Some posts being letters is a possibility — follows; it is definite
by conversion, and a definite truth always satisfies a possibility.
**Both I and II follow.**

**Example 2:** No cup is a plate. Some plates are dishes.
I. Some dishes being cups is a possibility — can a dish overlap the cup
circle while no cup touches a plate? Yes, dishes may sit anywhere outside
"plate". Not contradicted, so **true**.
II. Some cups are dishes — does **not** follow definitely; the overlap is
only possible, not necessary. **Only I follows.**

## 9. Strategy Summary

| Question phrasing | What it demands | Failure condition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| "definitely follows" | true in every Venn arrangement | one counter-arrangement kills it |
| "is a possibility" | true in at least one arrangement | contradicted in every arrangement |
| "either I or II follows" | complementary pair, neither definite | both follow, or the pair is not complementary |
| three statements | combine in pairs, then again | skipping the derived conclusions |

[example]
Exam method: **(1)** label each statement A/E/I/O, **(2)** apply the
conversion table if the conclusion's terms look reversed, **(3)** sketch
the overlap — "some" includes "all inside," **(4)** for possibility
questions, hunt for a contradiction, not a proof, **(5)** check
complementary pairs before marking "neither follows."
[/example]